# Part One — The Status of Women in Pakistan

# Chapter 1 · Measuring Women's Status: The Global Gender Gap and Pakistan

> Two questions asked why Pakistan ranks so low: "What reasons are responsible for ranking Pakistan as the **second-worst country** in the world for gender inequality despite… the 1973 Constitution?" (2018) and "Evaluate Pakistan's performance in the World Economic Forum's **Global Gender Gap Report 2025**… Identify the socio-economic and political factors" (2026). A 2024 MCQ asked Pakistan's rank in 2023 (**142nd of 146**).

## 1.1  How women's status is measured

Women's status cannot be seen in one number, so international bodies combine several indicators into an **index**.

::: table | The main international measures of women's status.
@widths 3.6,3,7.4
Index | Publisher | What it measures
Global Gender Gap Index (GGGI) | World Economic Forum, since ==2006== | Gaps between women and men in **economic participation and opportunity**, **educational attainment**, **health and survival**, and **political empowerment**
Gender Inequality Index (GII) | UNDP, since ==2010== | Reproductive health, empowerment and labour-market gaps
Gender Development Index (GDI) | UNDP, since ==1995== | Women's human development compared with men's
Women, Business and the Law | World Bank | Legal equality in areas such as work, pay, marriage and assets
SDG 5 indicators | UN | Progress on the gender goal's targets
:::

## 1.2  The Global Gender Gap Index explained

::: define Global Gender Gap Index | World Economic Forum
A yearly score from 0 to 1 (or 0–100%) showing how much of the **gap between women and men** a country has closed across four sub-indices: **economic participation and opportunity**, **educational attainment**, **health and survival**, and **political empowerment**. A score of 100% would mean full parity. It measures **gaps**, not absolute levels — a poor country can score well if women and men are equally poor.
:::

::: fig grid | The four sub-indices of the Global Gender Gap Index.
@cols 4
Economic participation: labour force, wages, income, senior roles, professional jobs
Educational attainment: literacy and enrolment at each level
Health and survival: sex ratio at birth, healthy life expectancy
Political empowerment: women in parliament, ministers, years with a woman head of state
:::

## 1.3  Pakistan's record

::: fig bars | Pakistan's rank in the Global Gender Gap Report (lower is better; out of the countries shown).
@colour 2F7D4F
2006 (of 115): 112
2017 (of 144): 143 *
2021 (of 156): 153
2023 (of 146): 142
2025 (of 148): 148 *
2026 (of 145): 143
:::

::: data Global Gender Gap Report 2025 | World Economic Forum
Pakistan ranked ==148th of 148== — **last** — with ==56.7%== of its gender gap closed (about 57% in 2024). The share of women among government ministers fell to **zero**. The report recorded a small rise in female literacy.
:::

::: data Global Gender Gap Report 2026 | World Economic Forum
Pakistan rose to ==143rd of 145==, with ==59.5%== of the gap closed — its strongest gain in a decade. Sub-index ranks: **economic participation** ==144th==, **educational attainment** ==130th==, **political empowerment** ==107th==. It was still the lowest of the seven South Asian countries and of 38 lower-middle-income economies. The global gap was ==69.2%== closed, with parity ==120 years== away; **Iceland** ranked first for the 17th year.
:::

In ==2017== Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 144== — "**second-worst**", the basis of the 2018 question.

## 1.3a  Reading Pakistan's record over time

When the index began in ==2006==, Pakistan ranked ==112th of 115==. Its **score** rose slowly over the next two decades as girls' enrolment and women's political representation grew — the **60 reserved seats** of ==2002== helped the political sub-index. But its **rank** often fell, because other countries improved faster and more countries were added. In ==2017== it was ==143rd of 144== ("second-worst"), in ==2021== ==153rd of 156==, in ==2023== ==142nd of 146== (MCQ 2024), and in ==2025== last of 148. The ==2026== jump to ==143rd of 145== came mainly from **education** and **political empowerment**, each up 11 places.

The lesson for an exam answer is to distinguish **rank** (relative to others) from **score** (absolute progress). Pakistan has made slow absolute progress, but others have moved faster.

## 1.4  Why Pakistan ranks so low — the four sub-indices

### (1) Economic participation — the biggest gap

- Female **labour-force participation** is only ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25), against roughly 80% for men.
- Women earn far less; the ILO has reported one of the **widest gender pay gaps** in the world for Pakistan.
- Very few women are **managers, legislators or senior officials**.
- Most working women are in **agriculture** and the **informal sector**, much of it unpaid.

### (2) Educational attainment

- Female literacy is about ==53%== against about ==68%== for men (Census 2023).
- More than **25 million** children are out of school, most of them girls.
- Gaps are widest in **rural** areas and in **Balochistan** and parts of **Khyber Pakhtunkhwa**.

### (3) Health and survival

- A **skewed sex ratio** and son preference; the maternal mortality ratio was ==186== (2019); high anaemia and malnutrition among women.

### (4) Political empowerment

- Women's presence in the National Assembly rests mainly on **60 reserved seats**; only ==11== women won **general** seats in ==2024==.
- In 2025 there were **no women ministers**; a woman became chief minister of Punjab in ==2024==.

## 1.5  "Despite the Constitution and conventions" — why commitments have not closed the gap (2018)

Pakistan's **Constitution** (Articles 25, 34) promises equality, and it has joined **CEDAW** (==1996==), the **Beijing Platform** (==1995==) and the **SDGs** (==2015==). Yet the gap persists. The reasons:

::: fig cycle | Why commitments have not closed Pakistan's gender gap.
@centre A persistent gender gap
Weak enforcement of laws
Patriarchal norms: purdah, honour, son preference
Poverty and low public spending on health and education
Unsafe transport and harassment limit mobility
Unpaid care and no childcare
Weak political will and representation
Conflict, disasters and economic crises
:::

1. **The enforcement gap.** Laws exist on paper; police, courts and local officials often do not apply them.
2. **Patriarchal social norms** — purdah, family honour, son preference, early marriage — limit girls' schooling, women's mobility and work.
3. **Poverty and low spending.** Public spending on health and education has long been low — each only about 1–2% of GDP — so services are weak, especially for rural women.
4. **Mobility and safety.** Unsafe transport and harassment keep women out of work and study.
5. **Unpaid care.** Without childcare, women carry most household and care work.
6. **Political will and voice.** Few women in elected office; gender often low on the agenda.
7. **Crises.** Conflict in the north-west (attacks on girls' schools), the ==2022== floods and economic crises hit women hardest.
8. **Reservations and uneven federalism.** After the **18th Amendment** (==2010==), women's development moved to the provinces, whose capacity and laws differ.

## 1.5a  Pakistan in South Asia

Pakistan has usually ranked **last in South Asia** in the Global Gender Gap Report. Neighbours with similar incomes and cultures have done better in particular areas:

- **Bangladesh** has had far higher female labour-force participation, driven by the export garment industry, and has long had women heads of government.
- **Sri Lanka** has near-universal female literacy and good maternal health, the result of decades of public spending on schools and clinics.
- **Nepal** raised women's political representation through quotas in its 2015 constitution.

The comparison shows that Pakistan's low ranking is **not fixed by religion or region**: policy choices — spending on girls' schools and clinics, jobs that women can reach safely, quotas and their enforcement — make the difference.

## 1.5b  What would move Pakistan up the index

Because the index measures **gaps**, Pakistan's rank would rise fastest with changes in the **economic** sub-index, where it is weakest.

1. **More women in paid work** — through safe transport, childcare, enforcement of the harassment law and home-based and digital work with protection.
2. **Closing the pay gap** — minimum wages enforced for women; equal pay for work of equal value.
3. **Women in senior posts** — in the civil service, business and boards.
4. **Girls' secondary education** — to close the enrolment gap after primary school.
5. **Maternal and child health** — skilled birth attendance and nutrition.
6. **Women ministers and elected women** — the 2025 fall to zero women ministers cost Pakistan heavily; appointing women to cabinet is one of the quickest ways to improve the political score.

::: simple
The index is like a report card with four subjects. Pakistan is failing worst in one subject — **economic participation**. Improving that subject would lift the overall grade more than anything else.
:::

## 1.6  Critiques of the index

::: balance | How far should we trust the ranking?
- **For:** it is consistent over time, uses official data, and compares countries on the same basis; it draws attention to Pakistan's economic gap.
- **Against:** it measures **gaps**, not well-being; small data changes can move ranks sharply; it misses **violence**, **unpaid care** and informal work; data for Pakistan can be dated.
-> **Line to take:** the ranking is a **signal**, not the whole story; Pakistan's rise in 2026 is welcome, but its economic gap remains among the world's widest.
:::

::: eye | The model structure for 2026 Q4
1. Introduction: the index, its four sub-indices; Pakistan 148/148 (56.7%) in 2025 and 143/145 (59.5%) in 2026. 2. Economic participation — LFPR 24.4%, pay gap, informal work. 3. Education — literacy 53/68, out-of-school girls. 4. Health — MMR 186, anaemia, sex ratio. 5. Political empowerment — 11 women on general seats, no women ministers in 2025. 6. Socio-economic factors (poverty, norms, mobility, care). 7. Political factors (will, enforcement, federalism). 8. Critique of the index. 9. Way forward. 10. Conclusion. Draw the bars figure.
:::
